#!/bin/sh
# The gate for a Go repository in the cafaye org. Download, build, test.
#
#   bin/prime
#
# This began as a copy of kit's Go template and has since DIVERGED from it in
# ways worth stating rather than leaving to be discovered. kit's
# templates/bin-prime/go.sh is `#!/usr/bin/env bash` with `set -euo pipefail`,
# a `--fast` argument, `require go`/`require git` preflight checks, `go vet`,
# and a closing `prime ok` line. This file is POSIX `/bin/sh`, takes no
# arguments, and runs three commands. That is a deliberate narrowing — the
# service needs none of kit's extras — EXCEPT that it also lost `-count=1`, which
# was not a narrowing but a hole, and which is restored below. `if kit changes
# it, follow kit` still holds, and this file is now closer to kit than it was.
#
# The shebang is load-bearing and it was MISSING from this file until
# gate.yml (packet identity-13-gate). This file has the executable bit and no
# interpreter line, so execve(2) on it fails with ENOEXEC. A shell running
# `./bin/prime` never notices — bash and zsh both fall back to interpreting a
# shebang-less file themselves — which is why a developer and CI were both green
# while `core`'s `harness/gate_check.py`, which runs the declared gate with
# subprocess and NO shell, could not start it at all:
#
#     FAIL gate.command-missing: gate.command could not be started:
#          [Errno 8] Exec format error: 'bin/prime'
#
# Every other bin/prime in the fleet carries an interpreter line, and so does
# kit's Go template (`#!/usr/bin/env bash`); this one lost its own at scaffold
# time in identity-01 and nothing noticed for twelve packets. `/bin/sh` and not
# bash: the body is three POSIX commands and `set -eu`, so the more portable
# interpreter is the honest one, and the divergence is recorded above.
set -eu

go mod download
go build ./...

# -count=1 defeats Go's test cache, and without it THIS GATE CAN BE GREEN
# WITHOUT RUNNING ANYTHING. Measured on this tree: with the cache warm, a plain
# `go test ./...` printed 18 `ok` package lines in 8 seconds and executed 2 of
# them — 16 read `(cached)`. Exit 0, every proof satisfied, a developer who had
# just run the gate had verified nothing. With -count=1 the same command runs
# all 18 packages, 0 cached, in 126s.
#
# This is not a local preference. kit's Go template ships
# `go test -count=1 ./...` (templates/bin-prime/go.sh:73) and says why in the
# line above it: "a prime must prove the tests run now, not that they ran at
# some point today". kit's validator REQUIRES the flag —
# `tests/validate.sh:3014` fails a Go CI step that omits it — and kit's reusable
# workflow passes it. This file was the fleet's Go gate without it, so identity's
# gate was the one place the rule did not hold.
go test -count=1 ./...
